# GGUF K-quant weights served as stored: a fully-connected op that decodes the blocks in its kernel

# Applied against the pinned runtime, build 2026.4.0-22849-71640275d29, on top of
# patches/0003-0020 (verified: applies cleanly on the pristine pin with exactly that
# series and builds the +p7 package from it). arcint 0.4.0 stage 1, docs/design-gguf-native.md (DESIGN.md section 7.0.2ay).
#
# What this is for. arcint opens a GGUF in process on the served IR of the same
# architecture as a topology template and hands every projection's weight to the
# plugin as the file's own bytes: a u8 constant [N, row_bytes] of Q4_K / Q5_K / Q6_K /
# Q8_0 super-blocks, laid out exactly as in the file. The operator's rule for the
# whole path: no unpack at load, no reorder at compile, no second copy of the weights
# in any format -- the only bytes on the card are the file's.
#
# What changes:
#   src/plugin/ops/fully_connected_kquant.cpp  A factory for the op arcint builds,
#                       "FullyConnectedKQuant" in "arcint_opset", recognised by TYPE NAME
#                       ONLY: the op class lives in arcint (it implements shape inference
#                       there), the plugin reads two runtime-info integers off the node
#                       (arcint_kquant_type = the ggml type id, arcint_kquant_k = the
#                       logical K) and the row count off the weights' shape. No class
#                       crosses the library boundary. Inputs: activation, weights, an
#                       optional bias.
#   primitives/fully_connected.hpp  Three fields on the primitive (kquant_type, kquant_k,
#                       kquant_n), hashed, compared, serialised.
#   graph/fully_connected.cpp  Shape inference sees the weights as [N, K] (the memory is
#                       [N, row_bytes]); a malformed request (a K that is not a whole
#                       number of blocks, a byte count that is not N rows) is refused
#                       HERE, on the main thread, as an exception the caller sees -- a
#                       missing kernel at impl selection would terminate the process from
#                       a worker thread, which the first version of this patch did.
#   impls/ocl/fully_connected.cpp  The kernel's weights tensor is pinned to [N, row_bytes]:
#                       the impl used to re-derive a 2-D weights shape as [bytes / K, K]
#                       from the activation's feature size, which handed the kernel 27 rows
#                       of 512 for 48 rows of 288 (measured, the first run of the tests).
#   impls/onednn/fully_connected_onednn.hpp  oneDNN declines K-quant primitives.
#   kernel_selector  weight_bias_params carries the three fields; the FC kernel base
#                       declines K-quant weights unless a kernel says SupportsKQuant();
#                       the new kernel fully_connected_gpu_kquant (kernels/fully_connected/
#                       fully_connected_kernel_kquant.{h,cpp}, cl_kernels/
#                       fully_connected_gpu_kquant.cl, include/batch_headers/
#                       kquant_utils.cl) asks for the weights' own layout so the compile-
#                       time reorder is a no-op and decodes super-blocks in its inner loop.
#                       One LANE per output column; two variants compiled from the one
#                       source and chosen at dispatch by the activation's row count:
#                         decode (one row): each lane walks its column's super-blocks,
#                         decodes a 32-value sub-block into registers and multiplies it
#                         with the row (one broadcast read per subgroup); K is split over
#                         four subgroups of a work-group, reduced through local memory.
#                         prefill (rows > 1): tiles of 32 rows; the decoded sub-block,
#                         converted to f16, is the lane's column of the B operand of the
#                         subgroup matrix multiply (XMX, intel_sub_group_f16_f16_matrix_
#                         mad_k16; subgroup 16 on Xe2, 8 before), the activation tile of a
#                         super-block is staged in local memory once per work-group of
#                         eight subgroups and read from there as the A operand. f32
#                         accumulation; bias, activation and eltwise fusions as the
#                         reference kernel; 2-D and 3-D activations; dynamic shapes.
#                       kquant_utils.cl is ggml's dequantize_row_* formula for formula
#                       (get_scale_min_k4 included), over 32-bit or 16-bit words of the
#                       block with a shift, a mask and a convert per value. Its reference
#                       in the tests below is a second, independent transcription of the
#                       same formulas (kq_host); a host compile of this source is owed.
#
# How the kernel got here, measured at the dense model's gate projection (N 17,408, K
# 5,120, Q4_K, 50 MB of rows; the DISABLED_ timing test prints these): eight
# versions, the 24 GB card, one launch --
#   one subgroup per column, lanes over K, byte-wise decode:    M=1 523 us (16 GB card), M=32 9.4 ms
#     -- private_size 256 in the compiled kernel: a run-time loop bound over the
#        accumulators sent them to scratch; the activation re-read per column.
#   lane per column, uniform activation loads, word decode:    M=1 234 us, M=2048 298 ms
#   + XMX for the tile, A operand from global memory:          M=1 234 us, M=2048 298 ms
#     -- dropping the A loads alone gave 11 ms: the tile re-read once per 16 columns.
#   + A tile staged in local memory per work-group (this one):  M=1 225 us (222 GB/s of
#        rows), M=32 0.95 ms, M=256 5.6 ms, M=2048 39.5 ms; 16 GB card 509 us / 78 ms.
#
# Red first: eleven correctness cases (Q4_K at M = 1, 37 and a 3-D 5; Q5_K; Q6_K at
# 1 and a 3-D 19; Q8_0; K over one to five super-blocks, N not a multiple of the
# subgroup) build random, structurally valid super-blocks, dequantize them on the host
# with ggml's formulas, and compare the kernel's output with a plain f32 matmul at
# 2e-3 relative plus, for the tiled variant, 2^-11 of the sum of |x_k w_k| (its
# weights are f16 copies) -- with the weights tensor mis-derived (above) every case
# failed with "could not find a suitable kernel"; all eleven pass on both cards, the
# refusal case is an exception at network build. The fully-connected suite is
# unchanged beside them.
#
# MEASURED end to end (arcint at the stage-1 commit, 24 GB card): the dense
# Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M file opens on the dense IR template -- 497 projections from the
# file (Q4_K x288, Q6_K x65, Q5_K x48, F32 x96), 14.94 GiB resident -- and scores
# 10/10 on the Prüfstand through the served endpoint. Rates, the same card, one fresh process per cell, u8 KV, one lane, MTP off,
# prefix cache off, the chunk the fit's choice per arm (256 for this path: it charges 2.65
# GiB of activations for the kernel's f32 outputs; 2048 for the IR): 213 t/s prefill and
# 9.9 t/s decode at 856 prompt tokens, 173.8 and 8.5 at 71,727, against Intel's own int4
# IR export of the same model at 1,609 / 23.1 and 551.7 / 16.5 -- prefill 3.2x to 7.6x
# slower, decode about 2x. No fault line in any cell. The first kernel served at 28.8
# and 3.5.
#
# What this patch does NOT do: the embedding gather (the template's i8 embedding stays,
# a later patch), the expert fusion for MoE files (stage 2), the sub-4-bit block types
# IQ4_XS / IQ3_S / IQ3_XXS / Q3_K (stage 3), any activation-side dynamic quantisation,
# 2-D block loads for the A operand (Xe2 only; the next step for prefill).
#
# Upstream: not yet filed.

